P-G4, spec 001 group 6 (W1; tasks.md T163, T164): the compose policy's shared state, for the Author's hand.

tools/gates/compose_policy.py is the gate's own tool, so its change is the Author's (tasks.md Format, "Patches"). It
applies after P-G3, whose host classes it extends: each hunk is against the text P-G3 leaves. Group 6 adds the tunnel
end's `shared-state` service to the one compose file, which P-G3's rules refuse as written. This patch admits it, by
name and exactly, and nothing else:
  - it joins edge, an ordinary bridge, to publish exactly 10.42.1.10:5432->5432, the private interface's fixed
    address, a literal no other host has, which the security group holds to EC2's group;
  - it binds /run/h4reet/shared-state (its key and certificate on tmpfs) read-only, and /srv/h4reet/shared-state (the
    data volume's mount) writable, its one writable bind outside the gateway's state;
  - it runs gateway-state's Postgres, the lock's tag and digest, copied to the account's registry as
    h4reet/shared-state (COPY_REPOSITORIES).
SERVICE_BINDS becomes a map of each service's binds to whether each may write. tools/gates/test/test_compose_policy.py
gets a case for each. On an export with P-G3 and P-G4: 182 passed; the patched policy PASS with 4 env files.

Built from plain copies (the text P-G3 leaves, into $TMPDIR), edited, then diff -u with a/ and b/ labels; never git
apply. Apply it on W1's -p branch after P-G3, with `patch -p1 < <this file>` at the repository's root. Until it is
applied, the branch's policy step refuses shared-state (3 refusals: its port, its network, its binds).
